\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesian Rupiah Problems Purely Political -Ex-IMF Neiss \b0\par\par\uc2 385 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 08:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The beleaguered Indonesian rupiah's problems are purely "political" and there is little that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can do to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , former International Monetary Fund Director Hubert Neiss said Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent moves to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and raise interest rates, though necessary, won't significantly boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Neiss told reporters on the sidelines of a banking seminar in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Neiss is currently Deutsche Bank's (G.DBK) chairman for Asia. \par \par \uc2  Last week, amid increased political uncertainty, the rupiah fell to 11,500 rupiah against the dollar, its lowest level since August 1998. It has since partially recovered and was quoted at IDR10,429 to the dollar at 0628 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Neiss said he doesn't expect Bank Indonesia to keep interest rates high for a long time as this would hurt the country's economy. \par \par \uc2  The former IMF official who was instrumental in formulating the multilateral agency's rescue package for Indonesia, however, declined to comment on whether Indonesia had made any recent progress in economic reforms suggested by the body. \par \par \uc2  "Both sides (Indonesia and IMF) are still engaged in discussion," he said. \par \par \uc2  Replying to questions from seminar delegates earlier, Neiss said though "Indonesia has had a bad press" due to its political situation, it has made "substantial progress in rehabilitating" the domestic banking sector. \par \par \uc2  "The priority of banks (now) should be to fully divest nonperforming assets," he said. \par \par \uc2  Neiss noted that despite Indonesia's woes, its economy was growing, albeit slowly, and has potential for faster growth in the future. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia "is still in a very difficult transition period," he said. \par \par \uc2  Replying to reporters' questions, Neiss said there was no risk of recession in Asia this year. \par \par \uc2  After a rapid 6%-8% growth last year, Asian economies will grow at a slower 2% to 4% rate this year, but that doesn't imply Asia is heading into a recession, he said. \par \par \uc2  Neiss said Asian nations are generally well positioned to avert another 1997-98 type of crisis as they have higher reserves, lower short-term debt and positive current account balances. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 21/03/01 07-01G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3l01bd2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
