\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  Rupiah Ends Up On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Despite Unrest \b0\par\par\uc2 160 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 10:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah closed higher Thursday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but traders said popular unrest weighed on sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Supporters of embattled President Abdurrahman Wahid blocked a port in East Java, Wahid's electoral heartland, to vent anger at attempts by his political opponents to unseat him. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 10,060 rupiah, down from IDR10,150 at Wednesday's finish. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrowed rupiah in the market to help the frail \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  via state bank dollar-selling was limited compared with previous days. \par \par \uc2  Investors remain bullish on the dollar amid the current political situation and fears of mass unrest triggered by efforts to topple Wahid. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah is expected to trade in a tight range Friday just above IDR10,000 to the U.S. unit. \par \par \uc2  -By Simon Montlake, Dow Jones Newswires; +6221 3983 1277; simon.montlake@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1159 1848 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3f0160k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
