\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Grains-Volatile rupiah defers Indonesian deals. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sambit Mohanty \par \uc2 532 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 07:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 14 (Reuters) - Wide \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations have prompted Indonesian commodity importers, who were eyeing soymeal, wheat and sugar shipments, to defer their buying plans, regional traders said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "No Indonesian buyer is ready to finalise contracts with the rupiah moving up and down," said a Singapore trader. "They are interested in some sugar and small amounts of soymeal but traders are deliberately staying away." \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah hit 30-month lows on Monday when it lost some 12.5 percent of its value against the U.S. dollar. Since then, it has recovered following \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "As it is, \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's meal and beans buying have been slow in the past few weeks because of excess stocks and early arrivals. But now, buyers are totally out of the market due to the rupiah," said a soymeal trader from Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia earlier this month bought 20,000 tonnes of soymeal from the United States at about $210 a tonne C&F for late March or early April shipment. Last week, traders were negotiating for another 30,000 tonnes of U.S. meal. \par \par \uc2  "It seems that the deal which was being negotiated has not materialised," said one trader. "The rupiah has been fluctuating since then. Even Indian meal is out of question now because of very high prices. I have not heard of any new contract for Argentine meal also." \par \par \uc2  According to traders, U.S. high-pro meal was offered to Indonesia at about $216 a tonne C&F. Indian meal was offered at $212 a tonne while Argentine low-pro meal was offered at $183. \par \par \uc2  "Even the unclear position of import duties is affecting any possible sales to Indonesia," said one trader. \par \par \uc2  Director General of International Trade Riyanto Yosokumoro said earlier this month Indonesia was considering raising import duties on rice, sugar and soybeans to protect local producers. \par \par \uc2  WHEAT, SUGAR IN SAME BOAT \par \par \uc2  Regional grain traders said wheat import contracts were also at a standstill because of the rupiah volatility. Not only buyers are staying away, but suppliers were also not very keen to aggressively push sales. \par \par \uc2  "The need for wheat in the domestic Indonesian market is still there but who is willing to take the risk at this moment?" asked one wheat trader. \par \par \uc2  Another trader said: "We are hoping that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stabilise soon. Maybe, from next month onwards we will see a few new wheat import contracts getting finalised." \par \par \uc2  Trade officials say Indonesia will likely see solid growth in wheat imports this year as food multinationals, buoyed by reviving growth, plan business expansions. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this month, Indonesia's biggest wheat importer and miller, PT Bogasari Flour Mills said it would buy another 100,000 tonnes of wheat from India in addition to the 100,000 tonnes it bought in January. \par \par \uc2  "One or two sugar cargoes to Indonesia could materialise this week but we are keeping our fingers crossed," said one Singapore-based sugar trader. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there have hardly been sugar sales to Indonesia after India sold three cargoes of 12,000 tonnes each late last month. Indonesia is Southeast Asia's largest sugar importer. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx3e045nd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
