\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up On Stronger Yen, Rupiah \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hasan Jafri \par \uc2 596 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 11:19\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly higher against the U.S. dollar late Tuesday as a stable yen and a stronger rupiah provided support to some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were reasonably calm despite the havoc on the regional stock markets following the sharp declines posted by U.S. equities overnight. \par \par \uc2  Stock markets across Asia were generally weaker with Singapore's Straits Times index shedding 2.8% and the Korea Composite Stock Price Index down 3.1%. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won, Singapore dollar, Indonesian rupiah, and Thai baht gained Tuesday, while the New Taiwan dollar and Philippine peso were slightly weaker. \par \par \uc2  "The yen strengthened, influencing sentiment," said Rebecca Patterson, global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The yen was quoted at Y120.17 against the dollar in late Asian trading, up from Y120.60 late Monday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The yen was spared a selloff in reaction to plummeting Japanese share prices Tuesday, as losses in the benchmark Nikkei 225 were within market expectations, players said. \par \par \uc2  Just how much the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market expected the Nikkei to drop wasn't clear, but the benchmark did shed 2.9% to close at 11,819.70, the lowest finish since January 1985. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won was one of the beneficiaries of the yen's stability. After opening weaker at 1,284.0 won to the dollar, the won strengthened throughout the day closing at KRW1,275.3, up from Monday's close of KRW1278.40. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, the New Taiwan dollar ended at a six-week closing low on\par concerns about foreign outflows from the equities market. Foreign\par investors Tuesday sold a net NT$2.57 billion in main board shares.\par - - 13/03/01 10-19G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Indonesian rupiah Tuesday rebounded sharply a day after it plunged\par below 11,000 rupiah to the dollar on political and economic concerns.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for the\par third straight trading day and the dollar closed at 10,200 rupiah, down\par from IDR10,500 at Monday's finish.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers in Singapore and Jakarta said the Indonesian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par entered a thin market and sold as much as US$70 million to defend the\par rupiah.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, the overall market sentiment remains bearish. Some players\par continue to bet that President Abdurrahman Wahid will have to consider\par some form of capital controls to shore up the rupiah.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For a change, the rupiah also provided the Singapore dollar with a\par breather.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Singapore dollar fell in early trading in a knee-jerk reaction to\par the declines in the equities markets but recovered in later trading,\par tracking the yen and rupiah.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Fears of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore also\par capped the U.S. dollar's rise against the Singapore dollar, dealers\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Singapore dollar was quoted at S$1.7594 to the U.S. unit in late\par trading, from S$1.7617 late Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Philippine peso closed lower in reaction to the slump in equities.\par The U.S. dollar closed at 48.160 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System,\par up from PHP48.145 Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Thai baht ended stronger against the U.S. dollar in Asia as\par investors unwound long-dollar positions following the retreat of the\par U.S. unit against other regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar finished at 43.55 baht, down from THB43.72 at the Asian\par close Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Hasan Jafri, Dow Jones Newswires; 65/421-4828;\par hasan.jafri@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3d015ji\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
