\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Dn Late On Rupiah, Yen Fall;Expected Weaker \b0\par\par\uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 09:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar tracked the rupiah and yen lower Monday, and remained pressured by demand for U.S. dollars from companies and banks even after the Indonesian and Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounced off their intraday lows. \par \par \uc2  Around 0807 GMT, the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7617, up from S$1.7571 late Friday in Asia. Against the rupiah and the yen, it was at IDR10,650 and Y120.40, respectively, compared with IDR11,500 and Y120.66 earlier Monday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar rose to an intraday high of S$1.7622 as the Indonesian and Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell amid growing political uncertainty in Indonesia and Japan. \par \par \uc2  With the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore making no effort to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , banks, investors and local companies continued to buy U.S. dollars at levels above S$1.7610 even after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retreated below IDR11,000 on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and retreated from its high against the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "A large government-linked bank was the main buyer of U.S. dollars when it moved above S$1.76, and almost everybody continued to buy (U.S. dollars) because the market is very bearish on regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that (the U.S. dollar) won't go below S$1.7610 shows there is very strong demand, and I won't be surprised if it goes up to S$1.7650 today (Monday)," he added. \par \par \uc2  However, a chief dealer at another bank said that market participants will likely hesitate to bid the U.S. dollar above the S$1.7620-30 range on fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the MAS. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said any retreat from the U.S. dollar's current level against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely cause the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be bought on dips by banks and companies that are keen to create a long position in it. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is expected to trade in a S$1.7580-S$1.7650 range overnight. \par \par \uc2  A weaker Singapore dollar and volatile regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  took their toll on Singapore government bond prices and pressured interbank interest rates higher. \par \par \uc2  Interest rate swaps rose sharply in line with bond yields. \par \par \uc2  The 10-year bond yield rose nine basis point to 3.77% and the seven-year yield ended ten basis point higher at 3.63%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the five-year government paper rose 11 basis points to 3.22% while the two-year bond yield ended 12 basis points higher at 2.36%. \par \par \uc2  These are pre-settlement closing bids and are subject to minor change. \par \par \uc2  In the interest rate swaps market, the most liquid seven-year swap offer was at 3.64%, compared with 3.55% late Friday, while the 10-year swap was at 3.89% compared with 3.79%. \par \par \uc2  The spread between the two- and 10-year swaps was at 126 basis points compared with 127 late Friday, resulting in a marginally flatter swap curve. \par \par \uc2  Weaker regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  squeezed liquidity in the interbank money market, pushing the overnight offered rate and term rates higher. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                    Late Monday       Late Friday\par                  bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight       3.7500%  4.0000%   3.5000%  3.6250%\par 3 Month         2.3125%  2.4375%   2.0000%  2.1250%\par 1 Year          2.3125%  2.4375%   2.1250%  2.2500%\par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakashdowjones.com\par -0- 12/03/01 09-54G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3c012hj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
