\par\par \b \uc2  WSJ.COM WRAP: Indonesian Rupiah Plunges Amid Protests \b0\par\par\uc2 1,077 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 14:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones News Service \par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  A WSJ.COM News Roundup \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Indonesia -(Dow Jones)- The ailing rupiah continued its freefall by closing nearly 4% lower Monday compared with Friday as political unrest fueled active dollar buying. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 10,500 rupiah Monday, up from its close at 10,100 rupiah Friday. \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, ended the session off the 30-month low of 11,500 rupiah it fell to earlier in the day due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Traders said they believed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent between $50 million and $60 million to defend the rupiah Monday, either by selling dollars indirectly through state banks or directly in the foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Still, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to lead to a sustained rally in the rupiah, which remains under pressure as thousands of student protesters Monday called for President Abdurrahman Wahid to resign. \par \par \uc2  The dollar last traded at this level in September 1998 at the height of the Asian financial crisis. Senior Economics Minister Rizal Ramli said that the government would not resort to capital controls despite the rupiah's plunge. "We aren't considering any sort of capital controls," Mr. Rizal said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  admitted its ability to support the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is limited because the plunge is mainly due to political instability. \par \par \uc2  At the heart of the problem is Mr. Wahid. Last week, the embattled Muslim cleric came under increased fire for remaining on an overseas excursion while ethnic conflict in the region of Kalimantan left more than 400 people dead. Mr. Wahid already is facing impeachment proceedings launched by Parliament in February over his alleged involvement in two corruption scandals. \par \par \uc2  Despite growing displeasure with his rule, however, the political uncertainty plaguing Indonesia is unlikely to end soon. Even Amien Rais, speaker of the country's highest legislative body and one of Mr. Wahid's fiercest critics, said last week that the impeachment proceedings will likely take several more months. \par \par \uc2  "The weakness is mostly because of political factors, so political parties should stop bickering," Deputy Governor Anwar Nasution told reporters. \par \par \uc2  The fall of the rupiah to levels not seen in more than two years underscores how seriously Indonesia lags behind other Asian countries in emerging from a region-wide financial crisis that began in 1997. While South Korea and Thailand, for example, can likely raise capital in international markets, Jakarta talks of issuing bonds backed not by the state but by receipts on natural-gas sales. South Korea at least made efforts to follow International Monetary Fund-favored reforms and push corporate restructuring, but Jakarta has been in almost a constant state of bickering with the IMF and has barely gotten corporate reform off the ground. \par \par \uc2  The tense relationship with the IMF is hurting investor confidence. The agency has been withholding a $400 million installment of its $5 billion support program to Jakarta since December because of disagreements on reform measures, and the standoff could sour Indonesia's Paris Club creditors and jeopardize an agreement reached last year to restructure $5.8 billion of foreign debt. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia's foreign reserves -- currently at $29 billion on a gross basis -- aren't large enough to defend the rupiah, Mr. Nasution said. \par \par \uc2  Normally, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  indirectly through state banks. However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold around $50 million directly Friday, but this hasn't helped the rupiah. Selling dollars directly to commercial banks is meant to give a signal to the market the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to defend the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Protesters Assemble at Wahid Palace \par \par \uc2  More than 10,000 demonstrators gathered in front of Mr. Wahid's white-column residence. Thousands more rallied at campuses across the capital and staged street marches. Scores of riot police and soldiers guarded the palace and kept the students apart from a small group of Wahid supporters. \par \par \uc2  Organizers of Monday's protests originally had called for a national strike. The capital was quiet, however, and many offices and stores were open as usual. \par \par \uc2  Following the student rally, the cabinet announced that the government has decided not to increase the price of "fuel for the people," including kerosene and leaded gasoline, but will consider raising it on Oct. 1 after consulting the House of Representatives. \par \par \uc2  "The government, however, has decided to increase fuel prices for industrial sectors by 50% and for foreign ships by 100% on April 1," Minister of Energy and Mineral Resources Purnomo Yusgiantoro told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Ordinary people usually use kerosene to cook, while public transportation uses leaded gasoline. The fuel-price increase is a sensitive issue that could lead to mass rioting. In 1998, it was regarded as a factor in the fall of Suharto. The government earlier planned to increase fuel prices by an average 20% on April 1 as recommended by the IMF. Without the increase, the government will have to cover a shortfall of 4.3 trillion rupiah in the budget. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Wahid later reiterated at a press conference that he wouldn't resign. "I will not resign," Mr. Wahid said. "The country will fall apart if I resign," he added. Mr. Wahid said he has urged his more popular vice president, Megawati Sukarnoputri, to take a more active role in government. \par \par \uc2  Under a power-sharing agreement last year, Ms. Megawati was supposed to take over the day-to-day running of the government. But members of her Indonesian Democratic Party of Struggle, or PDI-P, say the president has failed to hand over any meaningful power to their leader. \par \par \uc2  The pressure on Mr. Wahid rose in recent weeks following the slaughter of more than 450 migrant settlers at the hands of indigenous Dayak tribesmen in the province of Central Kalimantan. \par \par \uc2  In another restive province, Aceh, officials said Monday that separatist rebels attacked an oil depot owned by the state Pertamina petroleum company, blowing up several drums of oil but causing no injuries. Insurgents also killed two people in nearby Pidie, police said. \par \par \uc2  Over the weekend, Exxon Mobil's Indonesian subsidiary announced it had cut oil production in the region due to escalating fighting. But Mr. Yusgiantoro said the government will help guard ExxonMobil's facilities there. \par \par \uc2  The Jakarta Stock Exchange composite index fell 17.495, or 4.2%, to close at 396.611 Monday, off an intraday low of 391. \par \par \uc2  -- Wall Street Journal staff reporter Michael Schuman contributed to this article. \par \par \uc2 Dokument dj00000020010711dx3c02sgu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
