\par\par \b \uc2  Bk  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  Gov: To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  To Help IDR If Necessary \b0\par\par\uc2 262 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2001\par 07:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Sjahril Sabirin said Monday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupiah if necessary. \par \par \uc2  "If needed, we will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market," Sjahril told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Sjahril's comments come after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped below 9,800 rupiah against the dollar earlier Monday on local corporate dollar demand. IDR9,800 is the dollar's highest level since October 1998. \par \par \uc2  Sjahril said it wasn't clear whether the current weakness in the rupiah was temporary or will persist and require \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He didn't offer any levels when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Although he made the statement, dealers believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars through state banks earlier Monday. However, the amount was insignificant and failed to arrest the dollar's rise. \par \par \uc2  Concerns that the rupiah could weaken further, as international lenders toughen their stance on the Indonesian government, has fueled keen local corporate demand for dollars, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The World Bank warned Friday that it would cease all new lending to the government if the country's problems escalate into a political or economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  Market participants are also worried about the status of the International Monetary Fund's disbursement of a delayed $400 million loan tranche, as the government and the lending agency try to iron out differences over issues of fiscal decentralization and the controversial amendment of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, which could undermine Bank Indonesia's independence. \par \par \uc2  -By Farida Husna, Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; farida.husna@dowjones.com. \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx2q00u32\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
