\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia Grain-Arrivals peaking ahead of year-end. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Grace Nirang \par \uc2 427 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2000\par 06:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Nov 2 (Reuters) - At least 150,000 tonnes of U.S soybean and 200,000 tonnes of soymeal will arrive in Indonesia in November and December as traders and feedmillers stock up ahead of long holidays at the year-end, traders said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Indonesians celebrate Christmas and the Moslem Eid el-Fitr in December. \par \par \uc2  "One handymax ship and two Panamax will arrive in November to bring in 150,000 tonnes of U.S soybean. Around 120,000 tonnes of U.S soymeal will also come in November-December," said one trader in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  Another Jakarta trader said at least four vessels bringing in 100,000 tonnes of Indian soymeal were also expected to arrive in December. \par \par \uc2  "Everyone wants their goods to arrive here by mid-December at the latest. They want to avoid long holidays because there will be a shortage of workers and they will have to spend more money," said the second trader. \par \par \uc2  Indonesian workers normally go back to their hometowns to celebrate religious festivities. \par \par \uc2  But one trader in Surabaya said some of the Indian soymeal might not be able to reach Indonesia by mid-December because the Indian exporters were facing difficulties meeting the deadline. \par \par \uc2  "I think half of the amount will be delivered in January. The importers do not want the soymeal to come after mid-December, so they will delay the shipments," said the Surabaya trader. \par \par \uc2  Weak rupiah and rising demand ahead of year-end religious festivities have boosted local prices of soybean, soymeal and corn, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Corn rose to 975-1,050 rupiah/kg in Jakarta against last week's 925 rupiah/kg. Soymeal rose to 2,150-2,200 rupiah/kg. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was quoted at 9,150/9,165 rupiah against the dollar by 0420 GMT. The battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has come under pressure in the past few days on worries over the country's political stability. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said on Thursday it would keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Traders said rice supplies were abundant in Indonesia and saw no signs of fresh imports at least until January, although some rice from old contracts had yet to arrive. \par \par \uc2  "I don't see private traders signing new contracts since August. Bulog's warehouses are full of rice," said the first Jakarta trader, referring to state commodity regulator Bulog. \par \par \uc2  Bulog had earlier said it would not sign any more rice import contracts due to a glut in domestic supply. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwb202p4v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
