\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Southeast Asia's  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  woes, 1997 and all that. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2000\par 05:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 14 (Reuters) - The gloom shrouding Southeast Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of the rupiah's steep drop has spurred talk in the markets of a contagion effect, but analysts are quick to dismiss the notion. \par \par \uc2  This is not 1997, there is no balance of payments crisis and investors are not embarking on a 'sell-Asia' spree. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah's descent to 16-month lows last week amid mounting worries about Indonesia's political and security situation did spur some weakness in the Philippine peso, Thai baht and even the stalwart Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  But analysts note these declines are very much more tied to domestic ills or, in the case of Singapore, its trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. \par \par \uc2  "Simply viewing the recent weakness in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as contagion is missing the fact that the recent trends in the baht and peso were merely continuations of primary trends of weakness," said Desmond Supple, economist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  "While sentiment in Asia inevitably suffers from political instability in Indonesia, the actual impact on the regional business cycle is minimal," he said. \par \par \uc2  With healthy current account surpluses, floating exchange rates and debt restructuring efforts, there is little risk of the herd-like sell-off that characterised the Asian crisis, resulting in forced devaluations and near economic collapse. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said governments had clearly learned the lesson of '97 and were opting to let \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  take the strain, avoiding expensive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or economically-damaging rate hikes. \par \par \uc2  NOT 1997 \par \par \uc2  "The main point here is event risk has fallen in the region because all these economies have moved from fixed to floating exchange rates," said Vincent Low, strategist at Merrill Lynch. \par \par \uc2  "So moving from 37 to 40 (baht to the dollar) is very far removed from the days of the crisis when the Bank of Thailand's hand was forced and it had to go from 25 to 56 in a few months." \par \par \uc2  Low noted that Asia's macroeconomic profile was also much improved from before the crisis. \par \par \uc2  "We're talking about current account surpluses, companies that are less leveraged, short-term \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure has declined, easy monetary conditions". \par \par \uc2  Analysts said investors were, if anything, tending to differentiate more among individual economies in Asia, as evidenced by continuing portfolio inflows to Hong Kong, South Korea and Taiwan. \par \par \uc2  Even the Singapore dollar's fall to 16-month lows this week has more to do with the authorities condoning a mild depreciation as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its neighbours and export rivals slide. \par \par \uc2  In any case, the Sing dollar's 4.4 percent drop against the U.S. dollar since the start of the year is far removed from the peso's 10 percent loss and the rupiah's 27 percent slump. \par \par \uc2  REAL PROBLEMS \par \par \uc2  None of this detracts from the fact that Indonesia and the Philippines do face a very real threat unless they are somehow able to restore political and economic stability. \par \par \uc2  Both countries have been hard hit by doubts about their leadership, which have increasingly undermined their markets and economies, giving investor confidence a further jolt. \par \par \uc2  Thailand has also suffered a bit as opposition efforts to push for an early election spark worries about a delay to the national budget, but analysts say the risks are more contained. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like the Bank of Thailand, has opted for a hands-off approach, which analysts see as appropriate given still subdued inflation and efforts to manage foreign debt. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also ruled out \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it has repeatedly hiked rates since May, pushing one-month \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  certificates to 13.1 percent from 10.9 percent on May 10. \par \par \uc2  Unfortunately, that hasn't really helped the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  "Once everybody starts selling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the loss in confidence is much more overwhelming than anything that interest rates could do," said Mangal Goswami, regional economist at ABN-Amro Bank. \par \par \uc2  "Something has got to be resolved on the political front." \par \par \uc2  For Bank Indonesia, whose chief is himself embroiled in a political scandal, the options are less clear cut. \par \par \uc2  A sustained rupiah depreciation would inflate food prices, potentially sparking the kind of social unrest that led to former president Suharto being ousted in 1998. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, higher rates would magnify the cost of recapitalising the banking sector, already estimated at over 40 percent of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  "Higher interest rates are clearly an imperfect and incomplete solution to Indonesia's woes," said Steve Brice, treasury economist at Standard Chartered, in a research note. \par \par \uc2  "But those looking for perfect solutions will likely be disappointed." \par \par \uc2  ((Sonali Desai +65-870-3090; e-mail sonali.desai@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw7e01rsz\par }\page {\par\fs2