\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Early; Market Awaits US Econ Data \b0\par\par\uc2 553 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2000\par 03:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are mixed early Tuesday in cautious trade as market participants await the release of key U.S. economic data and the Federal Reserve chairman's assessment of the economy, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Further signs of a slowdown in the U.S. economy could weigh on the dollar as it may prompt the Fed to be less aggressive in raising interest rates this year, they added. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday, U.S. consumer price index data for May will be issued. \par \par \uc2  Market participants will also be seeking more clues on the U.S. economy and where rates are headed when Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan speaks later Tuesday at a business economics conference. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won has taken centerstage in the Asian market, as a 1.6% slide in the Seoul stock market damps the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The won is also coming under pressure on newspaper reports suggesting that international ratings agencies are unlikely to upgrade South Korea's ratings after the summit between North and South Korea, which starts Tuesday. Many had hoped for an upgrade as a successful summit would eventually reduce South Korea's country risk, market watchers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0135 GMT (9:35 p.m. EDT Monday), the dollar is at 1,114.40 won, up from Monday's close of KRW1,113.40. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has fallen to 42.38 pesos, from Friday's close of PHP42.465 as banks unwind their long dollar positions, dealers said. The Philippine market was closed Monday for a national holiday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers are unruffled by President Joseph Estrada's illness, which isn't expected to be serious. \par \par \uc2  Estrada is expected to continue undergoing medical tests after suffering considerable pain on his right side Monday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  rupiah was slightly stronger amid fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Sjahril Sabirin said Monday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market "every day" to support the rupiah, which authorities have repeatedly stressed in recent weeks is undervalued given the improving economy. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia is expected to sell dollars in small amounts near 8,650 rupiah via state banks, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has slipped to IDR8,575 from IDR8,608 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Corporate demand for dollars is expected to keep the dollar supported around IDR8,550 to IDR8,600, while long liquidation from offshore participants in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cap Tuesday's range around IDR8,670, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The market will be awaiting news on the International Monetary Fund's latest review of Indonesia progress in implementing reform pledges. \par \par \uc2  In Thailand, the dollar is at 38.965 baht, down from THB39.065 late Monday. The dollar will remain capped around THB39.10 on long liquidation in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by offshore players, dealers said. Local corporate demand for dollars will support the dollar around THB38.80, they added. \par \par \uc2  Against its Singapore counterpart, the dollar is at S$1.7220, down slightly from S$1.7235 late Monday. The pair will likely remained confined within its recent range of S$1.7200 to S$1.7250, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In Taiwan, the dollar is at NT$30.805, little changed from NT$30.802 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 421 4807 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw6d02wg3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
