\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia cen bank head against pure capital curbs. \b0\par\par\uc2 210 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2000\par 01:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 2 (Reuters) - Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Syahril Sabirin said on Friday he was opposed to outright capital controls but would be willing to discuss with the government "grey area" measures such as steps to control the flow of the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  In a brief telephone interview with CNN from Jakarta, monitored in Tokyo, Sabirin said he believed the rupiah on economic grounds was worth around 7,000 to the dollar or more. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah briefly breached the 8,400 level to the dollar on Thursday amid speculation capital controls would be introduced to protect the beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , hit by Indonesia's slow progess in reforming its economy and implementing political and corporate change. The rupiah later fell back to around 8,450, and was quoted near 8,500 early on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Sabirin said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the rupiah if it thought it would be helpful. But against a background of volatility, he said he did not want to waste the country's foreign exchnage reserves on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that might not aid the medium-term stability of the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  ((Alan Wheatley, Asian Economics Correspondent, Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8453 email: alan.wheatley@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw61041wp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
