\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Up Late As USD Surge Pauses;S$ Up On GDP \b0\par\par\uc2 526 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 2000\par 09:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were higher against the U.S. dollar late Friday as the U.S. unit took a pause from its recent rise, the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hiked rates and Singapore's government released stronger-than-expected growth figures. \par \par \uc2  Only the South Korean won stood out as a session loser partly due to the resignation of the South Korean Prime Minister Park Tae-joon, after he admitted to improper property transactions. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also pointed out recent falls in the local stock markets as the main reason for the won's depreciation. Local stock markets have fallen sharply since mid-April due to bearish U.S. markets and worries over the troubled local investment-trusts companies. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Korea Composite Stock Price Index has fallen 30.7% so far this year, while the over-the-counter Kosdaq index has fallen 46.8%. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 1,122.5 won, up from KRW1,118.50 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers reported only modest trading interest across the board. "It was like a holiday mood," said one dealer at a U.S. bank. Another Singapore trader said the market is waiting to see what Taiwanese President-elect Chen Shui-bien says in his inaugural address Saturday. \par \par \uc2  The upside for the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be limited, nonetheless, by the prospects for more rate hikes from the U.S. Federal Reserve in the coming months, said traders. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was up on news that the city-state's gross domestic product expanded 9.1% on year in the first quarter, above market expectations of just over 7%. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7285, down from S$1.7333 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A rate hike in the Philippines also boosted the peso slightly, with the U.S. dollar trading around 41.64 pesos, down from PHP41.73 late Thursday. With its key overnight rates hiked one half a percentage point, the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen as trying to prop up the local unit as the turmoil in the southern Philippines and the U.S. rate hike continued to undermine it. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was unmoved following the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's comments, ruling out more support for the country's beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar was quoted at 8,350 rupiah, down from IDR8,550 late the previous day. \par \par \uc2  "Only if needed, Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market; if not (needed) we don't want to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Sjahril Sabirin. \par \par \uc2  However, dealers said continued rupiah-buying by state-run banks helped cap the U.S. dollar at IDR8,500, and is partly behind the gains. \par \par \uc2  Against the Thai baht, the U.S. dollar was also lower, with the U.S. unit quoted at 39.135 baht, down from THB39.245 late Thursday. Dealers said the baht rose in line with other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was only slightly lower against the Taiwanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at NT$30.71, compared with NT$30.730 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  -By Eric Van Zant; (852) 2832 2340; eric.vanzant@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5j02gwb\par }\page {\par\fs20